Subject: [PATCH] drivers: i2c: stm32_v2: end the final reload segment in TC

Baseline: zephyr v3.6.0 (468eb56cf242eedba62006ee758700ee6148763f)
Applies to: drivers/i2c/i2c_ll_stm32_v2.c (interrupt mode) and
drivers/i2c/Kconfig.stm32. Independent of 0001 and 0003.

MEASURED ON DS20001, 2026-09-23 16:33. A VL53L7CX grid read is one
320-byte request, which this driver splits into 255 + 65 with reload mode.
For fourteen consecutive one-second windows, sampled from a timer interrupt
while every thread on the board was starved, the controller sat in exactly
this state:

  ISR   RXNE | TXE | BUSY, and TC, TCR, STOPF and NACKF all clear
  CR1   RXIE still enabled
  CR2   NBYTES 65, RELOAD still set
  driver current.len already 0, current.buf at 320 of 320

The event handler has no branch for that. It will not read RXDR, because
that is guarded by current.len. It will not issue a STOP, because no
completion flag is set. It will not mask the interrupt. So it returns with
RXNE still asserted on a level-triggered line and is re-entered about
890,000 times a second, against a healthy 35,500, until the watchdog
resets the board. The 500 ms transfer timeout does expire, in kernel time,
but its thread never runs again. No error flag is involved: BERR, ARLO,
OVR, PECERR, TIMEOUT and ALERT were all zero across 27 million interrupts.

Two changes, both about that state, and nothing else.

First, msg_init. Reloading a segment has to say whether another one
follows, and today it does not: when reload mode is already on it writes
NBYTES alone, so the LAST segment of a split transfer runs with RELOAD
still set and ends in TCR instead of TC. Both fields now move in one CR2
write. One write matters: setting NBYTES first and clearing RELOAD
afterwards leaves the reload live in between, which is the same race with
a smaller window.

Second, the event handler reads the status register back before returning.
The CPU and the NVIC are faster than the APB view of this peripheral, so a
flag cleared -- or a byte taken from RXDR -- a few instructions earlier may
not have reached it yet, and re-entering a level-triggered interrupt on a
stale flag with nothing left to do is the wedge above. The later upstream
rework of this driver adds the same synchronisation.

What this deliberately does NOT do is drain RXDR whenever current.len is 0.
That state is reached in ordinary operation about once per read -- 13.8
million times in the run that hung -- and is left again within
microseconds. Consuming a byte there would turn a normal segment boundary
into silent data corruption.

Both are behind CONFIG_I2C_STM32_LEXX_FINAL_RELOAD, default n, so the
production, E3 and L7-DEV images are byte for byte unchanged and the first
hardware run can differ from the run that hung in exactly one thing. The
guard is temporary: when the A/B passes it goes away and the change becomes
unconditional. The long-term answer is the upstream ISR rework, which
handles segmentation inside the interrupt; it cannot be cherry-picked onto
v3.6 as it stands and needs a contained backport that keeps this
integration's -ENXIO and -ETIMEDOUT classification.

---
--- a/drivers/i2c/i2c_ll_stm32_v2.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/i2c_ll_stm32_v2.c
@@ -50,7 +50,29 @@
 	I2C_TypeDef *i2c = cfg->i2c;
 
 	if (LL_I2C_IsEnabledReloadMode(i2c)) {
+#ifdef CONFIG_I2C_STM32_LEXX_FINAL_RELOAD
+		/* Reloading a segment must also say whether ANOTHER one follows. Writing NBYTES
+		 * while leaving RELOAD set makes the last segment of a split transfer end in TCR
+		 * instead of TC, and the controller keeps the transfer open with a byte that
+		 * software has already accounted for. Both fields therefore move in ONE CR2 write:
+		 * setting NBYTES first and clearing RELOAD afterwards leaves exactly the race this
+		 * is here to close, because the reload is live in between. START/STOP/NACK are
+		 * write-to-trigger, so they are masked out rather than written back.
+		 */
+		const bool more = !(msg->flags & I2C_MSG_STOP) && next_msg_flags &&
+				  !(*next_msg_flags & I2C_MSG_RESTART);
+		uint32_t cr2 = LL_I2C_ReadReg(i2c, CR2);
+
+		cr2 &= ~(I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Msk | I2C_CR2_RELOAD_Msk | I2C_CR2_START_Msk |
+			 I2C_CR2_STOP_Msk | I2C_CR2_NACK_Msk);
+		cr2 |= ((uint32_t)msg->len << I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Pos) & I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Msk;
+		if (more) {
+			cr2 |= I2C_CR2_RELOAD_Msk;
+		}
+		LL_I2C_WriteReg(i2c, CR2, cr2);
+#else
 		LL_I2C_SetTransferSize(i2c, msg->len);
+#endif
 	} else {
 		if (I2C_ADDR_10_BITS & data->dev_config) {
 			LL_I2C_SetMasterAddressingMode(i2c,
@@ -410,7 +432,7 @@
 		 * so send a stop condition manually
 		 */
 		LL_I2C_GenerateStopCondition(i2c);
-		return;
+		goto sync;
 	}
 
 	/* STOP received */
@@ -432,9 +454,20 @@
 		}
 	}
 
-	return;
+	goto sync;
 end:
 	stm32_i2c_master_mode_end(dev);
+sync:
+#ifdef CONFIG_I2C_STM32_LEXX_FINAL_RELOAD
+	/* Read the status back before leaving. The CPU and the NVIC are faster than the APB view
+	 * of this peripheral, so a flag cleared (or a byte taken from RXDR) a few instructions ago
+	 * may not have reached it yet -- and this is a level-triggered interrupt, so being
+	 * re-entered on a stale flag with nothing left to do is precisely the wedge this is about.
+	 * The read costs one APB access and makes the clear visible before the ISR can run again.
+	 */
+	(void)LL_I2C_ReadReg(i2c, ISR);
+#endif
+	return;
 }
 
 static int stm32_i2c_error(const struct device *dev)
--- a/drivers/i2c/Kconfig.stm32
+++ b/drivers/i2c/Kconfig.stm32
@@ -73,4 +73,26 @@
 	help
 	  Enable STM32 driver bus recovery support via GPIO bitbanging.
 
+config I2C_STM32_LEXX_FINAL_RELOAD
+	bool "DEV: end the final reload segment in TC, and sync the ISR before returning"
+	depends on I2C_STM32_V2 && I2C_STM32_INTERRUPT
+	default n
+	help
+	  DEV ONLY until the hardware A/B passes, then this guard goes away
+	  and the change becomes unconditional.
+
+	  Two related corrections to the interrupt-mode controller. First, a
+	  reloaded segment writes NBYTES and RELOAD in one CR2 write, so the
+	  last segment of a transfer split at 255 bytes ends in TC rather
+	  than TCR. Second, the event handler reads the status register back
+	  before returning, so a cleared flag has crossed the peripheral bus
+	  before the level-triggered interrupt can re-enter on it.
+
+	  Measured on DS20001 2026-09-23: without these, about one L7 read in
+	  23000 ends with RXNE asserted, NBYTES/RELOAD still describing a
+	  reload, the driver's byte count already zero and no completion flag
+	  to act on. The handler can then neither consume nor mask the
+	  interrupt, which re-enters about 890000 times a second and starves
+	  every thread on the board.
+
 endif # I2C_STM32
